;; vec-any-monomorph-is-half-plumbed, Half 2: a `vec-of` producing a `(Vec any)`.
;;
;; This is the route that did NOT clear run.sh's emitted-C ratchet.  The zero-
;; argument `(vec-new)` inside `vec-empty-like__`'s `any` clone resolved to
;; `vec_new__spec__tur_adt_Vec__int__` -- the SIBLING clone -- whose return ABI
;; is `tur_adt_Vec__int *` where this body returns `tur_adt_Vec__any *`, so the
;; build emitted `-Wincompatible-pointer-types`.  The ratchet was sweep-verified
;; at ZERO across the corpus before it landed, so that is a regression signal.
;;
;; The cause was not the lookup-side cross-spec fallback (which is where reading
;; the call sites pointed, twice) but a MISSING SPEC one layer up: the family-
;; element rehydration in emit_abi_register_call admits a scalar layout or a
;; concrete ADT app, and `any` is neither -- the layout table rejects TY_ANY on
;; purpose.  So no `{A -> any}` binding was synthesized, the call fell through
;; the `n_bindings == 0` gate, and the `any` clone never interned its own
;; `vec-new` monomorph for the fallback to find.
;;
;; This fixture is the heterogeneous shape S6/G7 needs: `[1 "two" 7.1]` in a
;; Saffron file lowers to exactly this `vec-of`, and each element must read back
;; with its OWN tag rather than the vector's.
;;
;; requires.leak-check: the element boxes are owned by the vector and released
;; with it.

(defn dyn [x : any] : any x)

(defn main [] : int
  (let [v (vec-of (dyn 1) (dyn "two") (dyn 7.1))]
    (println (vec-len v))
    (println (type-of (vec-get v 0)))
    (println (type-of (vec-get v 1)))
    (println (type-of (vec-get v 2)))
    (vec-free v))
  0)
